Context for your review: this module is part of a speculative decoding pipeline. The n-gram cache is used to draft candidate tokens; a match at the longest n-gram wins, backing off as necessary. The cache is persisted so that subsequent runs benefit from previously-seen text. A typical workload sees prompt reuse across many generations, so persistence matters. Empirically, ngram_max=4 and ngram_min=1 are good defaults; larger n_max tends to reduce recall, smaller n_min tends to reduce precision. For very repetitive corpora (code, documents with quoted text, chat logs containing earlier messages), acceptance rates of 40-70 percent are common. For more creative text, acceptance rates of 20-30 percent are typical.

Please structure your review as:

1. Correctness — any bugs, edge cases, off-by-one errors, error handling.
2. Performance — hot-path cost, memory footprint, asymptotic complexity.
3. Readability — naming, docstrings, structure.
4. API design — are the public methods well-chosen, is the persistence format reasonable.
5. Suggested changes — prioritised list.

Then repeat the module verbatim.

Additional notes for the review context — consider these when writing each section:

- The cache uses a defaultdict of Counter, so inserting a key that is absent is implicit. This is convenient but can hide bugs where an unexpected key is queried. A stricter variant would raise or return a typed "miss" object. Assess whether the implicit behavior is a good fit for this use case.
- The save() method serialises int keys as strings (JSON limitation) and the load() method casts them back. Any bug in that round-trip will silently corrupt the cache. Recommend a round-trip test.
- The update() method has a subtle off-by-one: `for i in range(len(tokens) - n)` excludes the last valid starting index when `i + n == len(tokens) - 1 + 1`. Verify whether this is correct or whether the loop should be `range(len(tokens) - n + 1)` with the "next token" fetched defensively (currently tokens[i + n] would be out of range at the last valid start).
- Performance: the defaultdict-of-Counter structure is O(1) per insert but has high per-entry overhead. For million-token corpora, a flat array of (hash, next, count) tuples, or a compact C-level structure (e.g., numpy arrays) would dominate. Assess whether this matters for the target workload.
- Memory: the cache stores every unique n-gram. For large ngram_max (say 6) on a 1M token corpus this can balloon quickly. Suggest a top-K pruning option that keeps only the most common entries per key. This trades a small recall loss for bounded memory.
- API: there is no clear() method and no way to cap the cache size. Consider adding both, particularly if the cache is intended to be long-lived.

